  "rationale": "The requirement asks for one structural item: `impl Sources { pub async fn search_structure(&self, query: &StructuralQuery) -> Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError> }`.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[search_structure]` matches it exactly. It is a `pub async fn` in `impl Sources`, takes `&self` and `query: &StructuralQuery`, and returns `Result<StructuralMatches, SourceError>`.\n- The supporting types exist in the same file. These are `struct[Sources]`, `struct[StructuralQuery]`, `type[StructuralMatches]` (an alias for `SearchResults<StructuralMatch>`) and `enum[SourceError]`.\n- The public method delegates to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#impl[SourceAnalysis<P>]/fn[search_structure]`. That method is generic over the provider and returns `P::Error`. `Sources.analysis` is a `SourceAnalysis<LocalSources>`, so the delegation only compiles if `LocalSources`'s error type is `SourceError`. I did not read that associated type directly.\n- The delegate in `sources.rs` is a generic internal helper. It is not a second signature that conflicts with the requirement, and it is reached from the marked public method.\n\nThe requirement states only this signature. It names no behavioral or testing obligations, and no ambiguities or unavailable information affect the result.",
